17. Look, physics and records - games/tilt-maze/, games/cosmic-voyager/,
    games/echodrift/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026. None of the three needed a licensing change:
the names "Tilt Maze", "Cosmic Voyager" and "EchoDrift", the folder names, the
ids, the i18n prefixes (tlt*, voyager*, drift*), the mazes, the asteroid field,
the sonar levels and every drawn shape are this project's own, generated or
drawn at runtime. No third-party art, audio file, font file or code is used;
Cosmic Voyager's sounds are synthesised by the Web Audio API at runtime.

games/tilt-maze/ was rebuilt. Its description called it a tilt-controlled
marble maze, but the old version had no tilt at all - only a joystick. The
rebuilt game asks for the device-orientation permission when the player picks
"Tilt" on the card and then really rolls the marble by tilting, with the
joystick kept as the default and as the fallback when a device or browser
refuses. The board now scales to the window on a device-pixel-ratio canvas
(it used to be a fixed 320 x 320 canvas stretched by CSS), the friction is
converted to a per-second value so the roll is the same at any refresh rate,
the collision step is subdivided so a fast marble cannot pass through a wall,
and the best score is kept per difficulty instead of one shared number. Pause,
the resume countdown and the 12-language wiring follow the rest of the
collection. Six i18n keys that only duplicated shared ones (tltDifficulty,
tltEasy, tltMedium, tltHard, tltScore, tltBest) were removed from all twelve
language blocks.

games/cosmic-voyager/ kept its own engine and art. Its loop ran one update per
animation frame, so the game ran roughly twice as fast on a 120 Hz phone; it
now runs a fixed 60 Hz accumulator, so the logic ticks sixty times a second
whatever the display does. The canvas is device-pixel-ratio aware and its
backing store now matches its displayed size exactly (before, the buffer was
sized from the parent box while CSS displayed it narrower, which both blurred
the picture and skewed pointer aiming). A pause button, Escape/P and pausing on
lost focus were added, and the score, best score, start screen and game-over
screen were restyled into the collection's glass pills and gradient-bordered
card.

games/echodrift/ already had a device-pixel-ratio canvas, delta-time movement
and a pause, so it kept its engine and got the collection's look plus the one
thing it lacked: a record. It now stores the best star rating for each of its
three levels and shows both the level's record in the HUD and the total
collected on every overlay. Its overlay was also a fixed 430-pixel box; it is
now a full-screen backdrop with the card centred in it, which fixes a card
that sat off-centre on wide screens and one that slid to the left edge on
short ones.

18. Sharpness, fixed step and records - games/twin-drop/, games/gem-swap/,
    games/bubble-burst/, games/snip-rope/, games/wobble-tower/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026. None of the five needed a licensing change and
none was renamed: "Twin Drop", "Gem Swap", "Bubble Burst", "Snip Rope" and
"Wobble Tower" are this project's own titles, as are the folder names, the ids
and the i18n prefixes (twd*, gsw*, bub*, snp*, wob*). Every board, gem, bubble,
rope and block is drawn at runtime by this project's own code, and each game's
scoring was invented here, not taken from the commercial games of the same
genres - twelve points per cleared block plus fifty per chain step in Twin
Drop, base points plus fifty per chain in Gem Swap, twelve per popped bubble
and eighteen per dropped one in Bubble Burst, a hundred and fifty minus thirty
per extra attempt in Snip Rope, and twenty-five plus three per combo for a
perfect landing in Wobble Tower.

What was fixed in all five:

* Sharpness. Every one of them drew into a fixed-size canvas that CSS then
  stretched, so the picture was soft on any phone. The grid games (Twin Drop,
  Gem Swap) now size their cell in real device pixels, and the fixed-space
  games (Bubble Burst, Snip Rope, Wobble Tower) keep their logical coordinate
  space and scale it onto a device-pixel-ratio canvas. Pointer mapping was
  rewritten to match, and the displayed size is now exactly the drawn size, so
  nothing is stretched in one axis (checked at six window sizes).

* Frame rate. Bubble Burst and Wobble Tower advanced their physics once per
  animation frame, so they ran about twice as fast on a 120 Hz phone. Both now
  run a fixed 60 Hz accumulator. Twin Drop and Gem Swap were already
  time-based, and Snip Rope's physics is a fixed 1/60 step that had to stay
  untouched: its levels are pre-verified by a solver, and the cut window is
  stored per level, so any change to the step would invalidate them.

* Records. All five kept one shared best score across difficulties or board
  sizes; each now keeps a separate record per setting and reloads it when the
  setting changes.

* Pause. Snip Rope had none at all - it now has a pause button, Escape/P and a
  veil, and the four that had a pause now also pause when the window loses
  focus.

* Small screens. The shared old panel layout left the game area a strip on a
  phone in landscape - Bubble Burst was reduced to a fifty-pixel-tall field by
  a hardcoded "100vh - 350px" cap. The cap is now proportional, the panel turns
  into a row on short landscape screens so the board gets the full height, the
  data strip becomes compact, and the board no longer pushes the panel wider
  than the screen. Twin Drop's board grew from 132 to 276 pixels on a 375-wide
  phone as a result.

* Look. All five were moved to the collection's typography, glass cards,
  segmented controls and pill buttons.

