21. Logic grids - games/sum-cross/ (renamed), games/shade-grid/,
    games/sign-grid/, games/binary-grid/, games/blackout-grid/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026.

Rename. The folder games/kakuro-cross/ became games/sum-cross/, the id
"kakuro-cross" became "sum-cross" and the i18n prefix kak* became smc*. The
displayed title was already this project's own ("Sum Cross"), but "Kakuro" is a
name coined and held by a Japanese puzzle publisher, and rule 2 in CLAUDE.md
requires a rename to go all the way through - displayed title, id, folder name
and key prefix. The stored best-score key moved with it. The number-sum puzzle
itself (fill runs with 1-9 so each run matches its clue, no repeat inside a
run) is a plain arithmetic idea and stays as it was; every level in the file is
generated and solver-checked by this project's own tools. The rename list in
CLAUDE.md now records Kakuro -> Sum Cross as well.

The other four needed no licensing change: "Shade Grid", "Sign Grid", "Binary
Grid" and "Blackout Grid" are this project's own titles for the shading, the
inequality, the binary-balance and the light-up grid ideas, and none of them
names a marked title anywhere - in the title, the id, the prefix or the
description.

games/binary-grid/ turned out to be broken, not just plain-looking. Its
generator built six valid rows (balanced, no three in a row, all different) and
then only tried to shuffle rows to remove column triples - it never checked
column balance at all. The resulting "solution" broke the column rules almost
every time, which made the puzzle unsolvable: following the hint button to the
end left the player with a full grid and the message that it breaks the rules.
The win check had the matching hole - it tested row balance but not column
balance, although the stated rules ask for both. Both are fixed: the generator
now searches over the fourteen valid rows with column constraints checked as it
goes, then removes cells one by one as long as the puzzle keeps exactly one
solution (with a floor of thirteen given cells, because a minimal 6x6 binary
puzzle is punishing), and the win check tests column balance too. Eight
generated puzzles in a row were verified against every rule, and a puzzle
filled from its own solution now wins.

games/binary-grid/ also had no record of any kind. It now times the run from
the first move and keeps the fastest hint-free solve, shown next to the row and
cell counters.

The rest of the batch: the two canvas games (Shade Grid, Sign Grid) keep their
logical coordinate space and scale it onto a device-pixel-ratio canvas - the
board grew from 278 to 333 pixels on a 375-pixel phone and to about 580 on a
tablet; Sum Cross and Blackout Grid now keep a best score per difficulty
instead of one shared number, Sum Cross sizes its cells from the panel width
and the window height instead of a fixed 46 pixels (it used to push the panel
