28. Gear Puzzle rebuilt - games/gear-puzzle/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 11 September 2026. No licensing change: the game carries this
project's own name, id and i18n prefix (gear*), and a train of meshing gears is
a machine, not anyone's property. Nothing about the rebuild touches the
collection's licensing position.

Why it was rebuilt. The idea was sound and is the only one of its kind here -
several cranks drive one machine, meshing gears turn opposite ways, and two
cranks fighting over the same gear jam it. What failed was that everything
explaining the puzzle was drawn at the edge of visibility while the decoration
was not: the lines showing which gears meshed were drawn at four per cent
opacity, the three roles were three shades of black, the symbols marking them
were nine-pixel type inside gears nineteen pixels across, and a win had to be
held for three seconds with nothing saying so. Worse, whether two gears meshed
was a distance test with an eight-and-a-half per cent tolerance, so on a real
board seven pairs out of thirty-six meshed and two more sat close enough to
look identical. The player could not see the graph the puzzle asked them to
reason about, so the only strategy left was clicking at random - and with three
cranks, seven of the twenty-seven combinations won, which made random clicking
work often enough to hide the problem.

What it is now. The gears sit on a square lattice, so only orthogonal
neighbours mesh and their teeth visibly interlock; the lattice is two-colourable
and the board is tinted like a chessboard, which is the rule itself made
visible - gears on the same colour turn the same way. Each machine on the board
is a separate group drawn on its own tinted plate, each crank has its own
colour, and a jam turns that whole machine red with a line naming the two
cranks by their colours. Every target gear now says which way it must turn, and
some machines carry a locked gear that must not turn at all, which gives the
crank's "stop" position a purpose. Those two marks together make the puzzle
solvable by reasoning - read the requirement, count the parity along the chain,
set each crank - instead of by guessing.

Generation is checked rather than hoped for. Every board is brute-forced over
all crank combinations and only accepted if it has a solution, if a jam is
possible somewhere, if it needs at least two moves, and if the share of winning
combinations is small enough that random clicking is not a strategy: a third on
easy, a seventh on medium, a tenth on hard. Across a hundred and twenty
generated boards none was unsolvable, and thirty of them were then solved
end-to-end through the real controls. The old code also had a silent failure
here - when it could not build a valid board it fell back to an easier setting
and, if that failed too, kept the last invalid attempt; the new one relaxes its
own checks in a defined order and never hands over an unverified board.

The board is also sized from the window and drawn at the device pixel ratio,
where the old one fixed its size once at load and ignored the display, and the
record is now the fewest moves per difficulty. Two i18n keys were added
(gearJam, gearLock) and the rule line and description were rewritten in all
twelve languages to state the new goal.
